<p>A distributed read needs more than a fast answer from every partition. The answers must belong to one permitted view of the database. Reading the stock before a purchase and the order after it can combine two individually valid observations into a state the purchase transaction never produced.</p>

<p>A common snapshot gives all participating reads the same history boundary. The database must choose that boundary, find the versions visible there, and establish that each replica can answer it safely. Timestamps help name the boundary. Their meaning and the evidence behind them matter more than the fact that they look like times.</p>

<p>We return to the purchase of order 103, with stock owned by partition A and the order and lines by B. This is the same deliberately split placement used in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/distributed-transactions/">Transactions across partitions</a>. The drawings use invented ordered timestamps to explain snapshots; no clock offsets, regional latency or native distributed execution are measured.</p>

<h2 id="choose-one-view-for-the-read">Common snapshots</h2>

<p>Before Mina’s purchase, P7 stock is 1 and order 103 is absent. After its successful transaction, stock is 0 and order 103 is pending, with its two agreed-price lines. P3 also changes from 12 to 10 in that transaction; the first drawing omits it so the two-read inconsistency is easy to see.</p>

<p>Imagine a report whose first query reads stock on A. The purchase then commits, and the report’s second query reads orders on B. If each query chooses a new current view, the report can see stock 1 and order 103 present. Atomic commit has not failed: the queries asked about different moments.</p>

